  </div>
  <p class="legend">
    <span class="planned">Planned</span> means on the
    <a href="#roadmap-title">roadmap</a>, not in a release yet.
  </p>
  <dl class="tools">
    <div>
      <dt>xmorpheo</dt>
      <dd>Rewrites the axes in the kernel, so every reader of the device
        gets the new values, from a configuration file with formulas.
        Attaching needs root, there are no buttons, layers, or GUI yet, see
        the <a href="#roadmap-title">roadmap</a>.</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Joystick Gremlin</dt>
      <dd>Maps physical devices to vJoy virtual joysticks, keyboard, and
        mouse on Windows, with curves, modes, macros, and Python scripts.
        Games bind to the virtual device, and HidHide can hide the real
        one.</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Steam Input</dt>
      <dd>Remaps controllers per game in the Steam client, with action
        sets, layers, and mode shifts, and presents an emulated controller
        to the game.</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>input-remapper</dt>
      <dd>Maps any input to keys, mouse, axes, or macros through virtual
        uinput devices, with a GUI and a system service.</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>evsieve</dt>
      <dd>Transforms evdev events on the command line and writes them to
        virtual uinput devices, for scripted setups.</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>jscal, evdev-joystick</dt>
      <dd>Calibrate the real device in the kernel: jscal the joydev
        interface, evdev-joystick the range, deadzone, and fuzz of the
        evdev axes.</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>AntiMicroX</dt>
      <dd>Maps gamepad input to keyboard and mouse, for games and
        applications without controller support.</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>udev-hid-bpf</dt>
      <dd>Loads HID-BPF programs on udev events, mostly fixes for specific
        devices. It uses the same kernel mechanism as xmorpheo, but each
        change is a BPF program written in C.</dd>
    </div>
    <div>
      <dt>Oversteer</dt>
      <dd>Sets the options that the drivers of steering wheels offer, like
        the rotation range and combined pedals, with a GUI.</dd>
    </div>
  </dl>
</section>

<section class="tested" aria-labelledby="tested-title">
  <h2 id="tested-title">Tested on real hardware</h2>
  <p>
    xmorpheo is not tied to a device or a game. It reads the report
    descriptor of any HID device and finds its axes. These devices are
    tested so far:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>VKB Gladiator EVO L and R, over USB.</li>
    <li>Xbox Wireless Controller (Series X|S) over Bluetooth LE with
      xpadneo.</li>
    <li>Thrustmaster T300 RS with hid-tmff2.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    xmorpheo is young: it works on these devices, but you may run into
    bugs, and the configuration format may still change before 1.0.
    <a href="https://github.com/xpadneo/xmorpheo/issues">Feedback and bug
    reports</a> are very welcome.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="start" aria-labelledby="start-title">
  <h2 id="start-title">Getting started</h2>
  <p>
    You need Linux 6.11 or newer with <code>CONFIG_HID_BPF</code> and BTF,
    and for building Rust, clang, bpftool, and the libelf and zlib
    development files.
  </p>
<pre><code>make
sudo make install
xmorpheo list
xmorpheo template 231d:0200 | sudo tee /etc/xmorpheo/conf.d/50-mystick.toml
xmorpheo attach --all --dry-run
sudo xmorpheo attach --all</code></pre>
  <p>
    Put the <code>VENDOR:PRODUCT</code> of your device from
    <code>xmorpheo list</code> in place of <code>231d:0200</code>, and give
    the axes in the template a curve.
    <a href="docs/INSTALL.html">Installing</a> and
    <a href="docs/CONFIG.html">the configuration</a> explain each step,
    <a href="docs/FORMULAS.html">Formulas</a> the curves.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="roadmap" aria-labelledby="roadmap-title">
  <h2 id="roadmap-title">Roadmap</h2>
  <p>
    What comes next, without dates and without promises. The
    <a href="https://github.com/xpadneo/xmorpheo/blob/main/docs/IDEAS.md">list of ideas</a>
    has the details.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Remapping buttons, hat switches, and D-pads is in development.</li>
    <li>Shift layers: a button that switches, or toggles, between mappings
      of the buttons and axes.</li>
    <li>Switching between curve sets at runtime, like a precision curve
      for aiming on a button press.</li>
    <li>Inverting force feedback together with an inverted steering
      axis, if the device protocol allows it.</li>
    <li>A graphical user interface that previews a curve on live input.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="caution" aria-labelledby="caution-title">
  <h2 id="caution-title">Force feedback devices</h2>
  <p>
    On force feedback devices such as wheels and joysticks, a wrong
    configuration, like an inverted or swapped axis, can make the game
    drive the device with full force against its stops. Depending on the
    device, this can damage the hardware or hurt hands, fingers, or thumbs.
    Test new configurations with the force feedback of the game turned
    down or off, and keep your hands clear. You use xmorpheo at your own
    risk, it comes without any warranty, see
    <a href="https://github.com/xpadneo/xmorpheo/blob/@REF@/README.md#safety-and-liability">Safety and Liability</a>.
  </p>
</section>

</main>

